Category to  and Type to All setting typesOffOnDisabledOffOwn companyAll companiesNoneOriginal	RealisticLeftCenterRight second␀␀␀sInfinite money: <Allow unlimited spending and disable bankruptcy of companiesMaximum initial loan: �Maximum amount a company can loan (without taking inflation into account). If set to "No loan", no money will be available unless provided by a Game Script or the "Infinite money" settingNo loanInterest rate: 7Loan interest rate; also controls inflation, if enabledRunning costs: ISet level of maintenance and running costs of vehicles and infrastructureLowMediumHighConstruction speed: 0Limit the amount of construction actions for AIsVehicle breakdowns: AControl how often inadequately maintained vehicles may break downSubsidy multiplier: /Set how much is paid for subsidized connectionsSubsidy duration: 6Set the number of years for which a subsidy is awarded8Set the number of periods for which a subsidy is awardedNo subsidiesConstruction costs: ,Set level of construction and purchase costsLowMediumHighRecessions: �If enabled, recessions may occur periodically. During a recession all production is significantly lower (it returns to previous level when the recession is over))Disallow train reversing in stations: �If enabled, trains will not reverse in non-terminus stations, even if there is a shorter path to their next destination when reversingDisasters: SToggle disasters which may occasionally block or destroy vehicles or infrastructureLocal authority attitude: �Choose how much noise and environmental damage by companies affect their town rating and further construction actions in the townMap height limit: mSet the maximum height of the map terrain. With "(auto)" a good value will be picked after terrain generation(auto)_You can't set the map height limit to this value. At least one mountain on the map is higher5Allow terraforming under buildings, tracks, etc.: CAllow terraforming under buildings and tracks without removing them3Allow more realistically sized catchment areas: SHave differently sized catchment areas for different types of stations and airportsNCompany stations can serve industries that have built-in neutral stations: �OWhen enabled, industries with attached stations (such as Oil Rigs) may also be served by company-owned stations built nearby. When disabled, these industries may only be served by their attached stations. Any nearby company stations won't be able to serve them, nor will the attached station serve anything else other than the industry@Allow removal of more town-owned roads, bridges and tunnels: @Make it easier to remove town-owned infrastructure and buildingsMaximum length of trains:  Set the maximum length of trains tile␀␀␀s#Amount of vehicle smoke/sparks: =Set how much smoke or how many sparks are emitted by vehiclesTrain acceleration model: ��Select the physics model for train acceleration. The "original" model penalizes slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalizes slopes and curves depending on various properties of the consist, like length and tractive effort$Road vehicle acceleration model: ��Select the physics model for road vehicle acceleration. The "original" model penalizes slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalizes slopes depending on various properties of the engine, for example 'tractive effort'Slope steepness for trains: \Steepness of a sloped tile for a train. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hill%&Slope steepness for road vehicles: cSteepness of a sloped tile for a road vehicle. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hill)Forbid trains from making 90° turns: ��90 degree turns occur when a horizontal track is directly followed by a vertical track piece on the adjacent tile, thus making the train turn by 90 degree when traversing the tile edge instead of the usual 45 degrees for other track combinations1Allow joining stations not directly adjacent: zAllow adding parts to a station without directly touching the existing parts, by Ctrl+Clicking while placing the new partsInflation: UEnable inflation in the economy, where costs are slightly faster rising than paymentsMaximum bridge length: #Maximum length for building bridgesMaximum bridge height: #Maximum height for building bridgesMaximum tunnel length: #Maximum length for building tunnels0Manual primary industry construction method: �EMethod of funding a primary industry. 'none' means it is not possible to fund any, 'prospecting' means funding is possible, but construction occurs in a random spot on the map and may as well fail, 'as other industries' means raw industries can be constructed by companies like processing industries in any position they likeNoneAs other industriesProspecting Flat area around industries: �Amount of flat space around an industry. This ensures empty space will remain available around an industry for building tracks, et cetera/Allow multiple similar industries per town: �Normally, a town does not want more than one industry of each type. With this setting, it will allow several industries of the same type in the same townShow signals: 2Select on which side of the track to place signalsOn the leftOn the driving sideOn the right0Show finances window at the end of the year: 2Show finances window at the end of the period: If enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each year to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the company�If enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each period to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the company)New orders are 'non-stop' by default: �9Normally, a vehicle will stop at every station it passes. By enabling this setting, it will drive through all stations on the way to its final destination without stopping. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can be set explicitly to either behavior nevertheless;New train orders stop by default at the  of the platform�_Place where a train will stop at the platform by default. The 'near end' means close to the entry point, 'middle' means in the middle of the platform, and 'far end' means far away from the entry point. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can have their stop location set by clicking on the order textnear endmiddlefar end)Pan window when mouse is at the edge: ZWhen enabled, viewports will start to scroll when the mouse is near the edge of the windowDisabledMain viewport, full-screen onlyMain viewportEvery viewport)Allow bribing of the local authority: �Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by an inspector, the company will not be able to act in the town for six months�Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by the police, the company will not be able to act in the town for six minutes,Allow buying exclusive transport rights: �If a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and freight) won't receive any cargo for twelve months�If a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and freight) won't receive any cargo for twelve minutesAllow funding buildings: =Allow companies to give money to towns for funding new houses,Allow funding local road reconstruction: jAllow companies to give money to towns for road reconstruction to sabotage road-based services in the town+Allow sending money to other companies: =Allow transfer of money between companies in multiplayer mode;Weight multiplier for freight to simulate heavy trains: �Set the impact of carrying freight in trains. A higher value makes carrying freight more demanding for trains, especially at hillsPlane speed factor: ySet the relative speed of planes compared to other vehicle types, to reduce the amount of income of transport by aircraft1 / Number of plane crashes: �Set the chance of a random aircraft crash happening.
* Large airplanes always have a risk of crashing when landing on small airportsNone*ReducedNormalCAllow grade crossings with roads or rails owned by competitors: LAllow construction of grade crossings on roads or rails owned by competitors;Allow drive-through road stops on roads owned by towns: FAllow construction of drive-through road stops on roads owned by townsAAllow drive-through road stops on roads owned by competitors: PAllow construction of drive-through road stops on roads owned by other companies@Changing this setting is not possible when there are vehiclesInfrastructure maintenance: �When enabled, infrastructure causes maintenance costs. The cost grows over-proportional with the network size, thus affecting bigger companies more than smaller onesStarting company color: %Choose starting color for the company%Starting company secondary color: RChoose starting secondary color for the company, if using a NewGRF that enables itAirports never expire: [Enabling this setting makes each airport type stay available forever after its introductionWarn if vehicle is lost: RTrigger messages about vehicles unable to find a path to their ordered destinationReview vehicles' orders: �When enabled, the orders of the vehicles are periodically checked, and some obvious issues are reported with a news message when detectedNo!Yes, but exclude stopped vehiclesOf all vehicles+Warn if a vehicle's income is negative: [When enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle has not made any profit within a year]When enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle has not made any profit within a period%Warn if a vehicle is getting old: DWhen enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle is getting oldVehicles never expire: RWhen enabled, all vehicle models remain available forever after their introductionTimekeeping: �LSelect the timekeeping units of the game. This cannot be changed later.

Calendar-based is the classic OpenTTD experience, with a year consisting of 12 months, and each month having 28-31 days.

In Wallclock-based time, cargo production and financials are instead based on one-minute increments, which is about as long as a 30 day month takes in Calendar-based mode. These are grouped into 12-minute periods, equivalent to a year in Calendar-based mode.

In either mode there is always a classic calendar, which is used for introduction dates of vehicles, houses, and other infrastructureCalendar	WallclockMinutes per year: �~Choose the number of minutes in a calendar year. The default is 12 minutes. Set to 0 to stop calendar time from changing. Increasing the length of the calendar year slows down the introduction of vehicles, houses, and other infrastructure. It does not affect vehicle speed or economic simulation, except for inflation. This setting is only available when using wallclock timekeeping0 (calendar time frozen) Scale town cargo production: 6Scale the cargo production of towns by this percentage$Scale industry cargo production: ;Scale the cargo production of industries by this percentage%'Autorenew vehicle when it gets old: sWhen enabled, a vehicle nearing its end of life gets automatically replaced when the renew conditions are fulfilled)Autorenew when vehicle is  maximum ageBRelative age when a vehicle should be considered for auto-renewing month␀␀␀s before month␀␀␀s after-Autorenew minimum needed money for renew: ^Minimal amount of money that must remain in the bank before considering auto-renewing vehiclesDuration of error message: �Duration for displaying error messages in a red window. Note that some (critical) error messages are not closed automatically after this time, but must be closed manuallyShow tooltips: �Delay before tooltips are displayed when hovering the mouse over some interface element. Alternatively tooltips are bound to the right mouse button when this value is set to 0"Hover for  millisecond␀␀␀sRight click0Show town population in the town name label: 9Display the population of towns in their label on the map!Thickness of lines in graphs: �Width of the line in the graphs. A thin line is more precisely readable, a thicker line is easier to see and colors are easier to distinguish7Show the NewGRF's name in the build vehicle window: \Add a line to the build vehicle window, showing which NewGRF the selected vehicle comes from?Show the cargos the vehicles can carry in the list windows: VIf enabled, the vehicle's transportable load will appear above it in the vehicle listsLandscape: �Landscapes define basic gameplay scenarios with different cargos and town growth requirements. NewGRF and Game Scripts allow finer control thoughLand generator: �The original generator depends on the base graphics set, and composes fixed landscape shapes. TerraGenesis is a Perlin noise based generator with finer control settingsOriginalTerraGenesisTerrain type: 9Choose the height of hills and mountains of the landscapeIndustry density: _Set how many industries should be generated and what level should be maintained during the game2Maximum distance from edge for Oil industries: ��Limit for how far from the map border oil refineries and oil rigs can be constructed. On island maps this ensures they are near the coast. On maps larger than 256 tiles, this value is scaled upSnow line height: ��Choose at what height snow starts in sub-arctic landscape. Snow also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Can only be modified via Scenario Editor or is otherwise calculated via "snow coverage"Snow coverage: ��Choose the approximate amount of snow on the sub-arctic landscape. Snow also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Only used during map generation. Sea level and coast tiles never have snow%Desert coverage: �Choose the approximate amount of desert on the tropical landscape. Desert also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Only used during map generation%Roughness of terrain: �Choose the shape and number of hills. Smooth landscapes have fewer, wider hills, while rough landscapes have more, smaller hillsVery SmoothSmoothRough
Very RoughVariety distribution: �Choose if the map contains both mountains and flat areas. The higher the variety, the more differences in elevation between mountainous and flat areasRiver amount: "Choose how many rivers to generateTree placer algorithm: zChoose the distribution of trees on the map: 'Original' plants trees uniformly scattered, 'Improved' plants them in groupsNoneOriginalImprovedRoad vehicles: Choose the driving sideDrive on leftDrive on rightHeightmap rotation: JChoose which way the heightmap image is rotated to fit into the game worldCounter-clockwise	Clockwise.The height level a flat scenario map gets: 7One or more tiles at the northern edge are not empty5One or more tiles at one of the edges is not waterMaximum station spread: iMaximum area the parts of a single station may be spread out on. Note that high values will slow the gameAPerform maintenance on helicopters at helipads automatically: RMaintain helicopters after every landing, even if there is no depot at the airport?Link landscape toolbar to rail/road/water/airport toolbars: `When opening a construction toolbar for a transport type, also open the toolbar for terraforming#Land color used in the minimap: $Color of the terrain in the smallmapGreen
Dark greenPurpleCargo flow overlay colors: 4Set the color scheme used for the cargo flow overlayGreen to red (original)Green to blueGray to red	GrayscaleViewport scroll behavior: �Behavior when scrolling the map. The "mouse position locked" options don't work on all systems, like web-based versions, touchscreens, Linux with Wayland, and others-Move viewport with RMB, mouse position locked(Move map with RMB, mouse position lockedMove map with RMBMove map with LMBSmooth viewport scrolling: ��Control how the main view scrolls to a specific position when clicking on the smallmap or when issuing a command to scroll to a specific object on the map. If enabled, the viewport scrolls smoothly, if disabled it jumps directly to the targeted spot0Traditional toolbar menu selection behavior: �Choose selection behavior of toolbar menus. If off, toolbar menus will stay open until a selection is made. If on, toolbar menus will activate when the mouse button is released.>Show a measurement tooltip when using various build-tools: ZDisplay tile-distances and height differences when dragging during construction operations(Show vehicle-type specific liveries: ^Control usage of vehicle-type specific liveries for vehicles (in contrary to company specific)NoneOwn companyAll companies"Prefer team chat with <ENTER>: TSwitch the binding of company-internal and public chat to <ENTER> resp. <Ctrl+ENTER>Map scrollwheel speed: 0Control the sensitivity of mouse-wheel scrollingFunction of scrollwheel: 2Enable scrolling with two-dimensional mouse-wheelsZoom map
Scroll mapOffOn screen keyboard: �Select the method to open the on screen keyboard for entering text into editboxes only using the pointing device. This is meant for small devices without actual keyboardDisabledDouble clickSingle click (when focused)Single click (immediately)Use relay service: �If creating a connection to the server fails, one can use a relay service to create a connection. "Never" disallows this, "ask" will ask first, "allow" will allow it without askingNeverAskAllowRight-click emulation: 6Select the method to emulate right mouse-button clicksCommand+Click
Ctrl+ClickOff Close window on right-click: QCloses a window by right-clicking inside it. Disables the tooltip on right-click!NoYesYes, except sticky*Use the  date format for savegame names)Format of the date in save game filenameslong (Dec 31st, 2008)short (12/31/2008)ISO (2008-12-31)1Automatically pause when starting a new game: nWhen enabled, the game will automatically pause when starting a new game, allowing for closer study of the mapWhen paused allow: 8Select what actions may be done while the game is paused
No actionsAll non-construction actions#All but landscape modifying actionsAll actionsUse groups in vehicle list: @Enable usage of the advanced vehicle lists for grouping vehiclesUse loading indicators: SSelect whether loading indicators are displayed above loading or unloading vehiclesTime units for timetables: 1Select the time units used for vehicle timetablesDaysSecondsTicks-Show arrival and departure in timetables: =Display anticipated arrival and departure times in timetables%Quick creation of vehicle orders: ;Pre-select the 'goto cursor' when opening the orders window1Default rail type (after new game/game load): ��Rail type to select after starting or loading a game. 'first available' selects the oldest type of tracks, 'last available' selects the newest type of tracks, and 'most used' selects the type which is currently most in useFirst availableLast available	Most used&Show path reservations for tracks: lGive reserved tracks a different color to assist in problems with trains refusing to enter path-based blocks+Keep building tools active after usage: AKeep the building tools for bridges, tunnels, etc. open after use;Automatically remove signals during track construction: �Automatically remove signals during track construction if the signals are in the way. Note that this can potentially lead to train crashesFast forward speed limit: ��Limit on how fast the game goes when fast forward is enabled. 0 = no limit (as fast as your computer allows). Values below 100% slow the game down. The upper-limit depends on the specification of your computer and can vary depending on the game% normal game speed*No limit (as fast as your computer allows)News ticker: 'Play sound for summarized news messagesNewspaper: %Play sound upon display of newspapersEnd of year: End of period: sPlay sound at the end of a year summarizing the company's performance during the year compared to the previous yearyPlay sound at the end of a period summarizing the company's performance during the period compared to the previous periodConstruction: 7Play sound on successful constructions or other actionsButton clicks: Beep when clicking buttonsDisasters/accidents: -Play sound effects of accidents and disastersVehicles: Play sound effects of vehiclesAmbient: 6Play ambient sounds of landscape, industries and towns)Maximum number of trains per company: 0Maximum number of trains that a company can have0Maximum number of road vehicles per company: 7Maximum number of road vehicles that a company can have+Maximum number of aircraft per company: 2Maximum number of aircraft that a company can have(Maximum number of ships per company: /Maximum number of ships that a company can have Disable trains for computer: LEnabling this setting makes building trains impossible for a computer player'Disable road vehicles for computer: SEnabling this setting makes building road vehicles impossible for a computer player"Disable aircraft for computer: NEnabling this setting makes building aircraft impossible for a computer playerDisable ships for computer: KEnabling this setting makes building ships impossible for a computer playerAllow AIs in multiplayer: =Allow AI computer players to participate in multiplayer games*#opcodes before scripts are suspended: FMaximum number of computation steps that a script can take in one turn Max memory usage per script: yHow much memory a single script may consume before it's forcibly terminated. This may need to be increased for large maps MiB*Maintenance intervals are in percents: �'When enabled, vehicles try to go for maintenance when their reliability drops by a given percentage of the maximum reliability.

For example, if a vehicle's maximum reliability is 90% and the maintenance interval is 20%, the vehicle will try to go for maintenance when it reaches 72% reliability,Default maintenance interval for trains: vSet the default maintenance interval for new rail vehicles, if no explicit maintenance interval is set for the vehicle3Default maintenance interval for road vehicles: vSet the default maintenance interval for new road vehicles, if no explicit maintenance interval is set for the vehicle.Default maintenance interval for aircraft: qSet the default maintenance interval for new aircraft, if no explicit maintenance interval is set for the vehicle+Default maintenance interval for ships: nSet the default maintenance interval for new ships, if no explicit maintenance interval is set for the vehicle Day␀␀␀s Minute␀␀␀s %Disabled8Disable maintenance when breakdowns are set to none: FWhen enabled, vehicles do not get maintained if they cannot break downFLoading speed penalty for trains that are longer than the station: �When enabled, trains which are too long for the station load more slowly than a train which fits the station. This setting does not affect pathfinding%Enable railroad car speed limits: WWhen enabled, also use speed limits of wagons for deciding the maximum speed of a trainDisable electric rails: gEnabling this setting disables the requirement to electrify tracks to make electric engines run on them1Arrival of first vehicle at player's station: LDisplay a newspaper when the first vehicle arrives at a new player's station5Arrival of first vehicle at competitor's station: PDisplay a newspaper when the first vehicle arrives at a new competitor's stationAccidents / disasters: 5Display a newspaper when accidents or disasters occur'Accidents of competitor's vehicles: :Display a newspaper about crashed vehicles for competitorsCompany information: ^Display a newspaper when a new company starts, or when companies are at risk of going bankruptOpening of industries: ,Display a newspaper when new industries openClosing of industries: .Display a newspaper when industries close downEconomy changes: 3Display a newspaper about global changes to economy;Production changes of industries served by the company: cDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are served by the company=Production changes of industries served by competitor(s): gDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are served by the competitors&Other industry production changes: vDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are not served by the company or competitors/Advice / information on company's vehicles: 1Display messages about vehicles needing attentionNew vehicles: =Display a newspaper when a new vehicle type becomes available Changes of cargo acceptance: BDisplay messages about stations changing acceptance of some cargosSubsidies: 0Display a newspaper about subsidy related eventsGeneral information: nDisplay newspaper about general events, such as purchase of exclusive rights or funding of road reconstructionOffSummaryFullColored news appears in: lYear that the newspaper announcements get printed in color. Before this year, it uses monochrome black/whiteStarting year: Scoring end year: �Year the game ends for scoring purposes. At the end of this year, the company's score is recorded and the high-score screen is displayed, but the players can continue playing after that.
If this is before the starting year, the high-score screen is never displayedNeverEconomy type: ��Smooth economy makes production changes more often, and in smaller steps. Frozen economy stops production changes and industry closures. This setting may have no effect if industry types are provided by a NewGRF.

Industry production per month may still appear to vary if the economy is Frozen, because production is triggered at fixed intervals which do not exactly correspond to months��Smooth economy makes production changes more often, and in smaller steps. Frozen economy stops production changes and industry closures. This setting may have no effect if industry types are provided by a NewGRF.

Industry production per minute may still appear to vary if the economy is Frozen, because production is triggered at fixed intervals which do not exactly correspond to minutesOriginalSmoothFrozen6Percentage of leg profit to pay in feeder systems: jPercentage of income given to the intermediate legs in feeder systems, giving more control over the income'When dragging, place signals every: }Set the distance at which signals will be built on a track up to the next obstacle (signal, junction), if signals are dragged tile␀␀␀s7When dragging, keep fixed distance between signals: �
Select the behavior of signal placement when Ctrl+dragging signals. If disabled, signals are placed around tunnels or bridges to avoid long stretches without signals. If enabled, signals are placed every n tiles, making alignment of signals at parallel tracks easier*Automatically build semaphores before: �Set the year when electric signals will be used for tracks. Before this year, non-electric signals will be used (which have the exact same function, but different looks)Cycle through signal types: dSelect which signal types to cycle through when Ctrl+Clicking on a built signal with the signal toolCurrent group onlyAll visibleShow signal types: 9Choose which signal types are shown in the signal toolbarPath signals onlyAll signalsRoad layout for new towns: $Layout for the road network of townsOriginalBetter roads2x2 grid3x3 gridRandom%Towns are allowed to build roads: iAllow towns to build roads for growth. Disable to prevent town authorities from building roads themselves/Towns are allowed to build grade crossings: ;Enabling this setting allows towns to build grade crossings1Limit airport placement based on noise level: �Allow towns to block airport construction based on their noise acceptance level, which is based on the town's population and airport size and distance. If this setting is disabled, towns allow only two airports unless the local authority attitude is set to "Permissive"Founding towns in game: CEnabling this setting allows players to found new towns in the game	ForbiddenAllowedAllowed, custom town layout#Placing individual town houses: BEnabling this setting allows players to place town houses manually	ForbiddenAllowedAllowed, fully constructedTown cargo generation: ��How much cargo is produced by houses in towns, relative to the overall population of the town.
Quadratic growth: A town twice the size generates four times as many passengers.
Linear growth: A town twice the size generates twice the amount of passengersQuadratic (original)LinearIn-game placement of trees: �Control random appearance of trees during the game. This might affect industries which rely on tree growth, for example lumber mills-Grow but don't spread (breaks lumber mill)#Grow but only spread in rainforestsGrow and spread everywhere0Don't grow, don't spread (breaks lumber mill)Position of main toolbar: @Horizontal position of the main toolbar at the top of the screenPosition of status bar: AHorizontal position of the status bar at the bottom of the screenWindow snap radius: aDistance between windows before the window being moved is automatically aligned to nearby windows pixel␀␀␀sDisabled)Maximum number of non-sticky windows: jNumber of non-sticky open windows before old windows get automatically closed to make room for new windowsDisabledMaximum zoom in level: oThe maximum zoom-in level for viewports. Note that enabling higher zoom-in levels increases memory requirementsMaximum zoom out level: ZThe maximum zoom-out level for viewports. Higher zoom-out levels might cause lag when used4x2xNormal2x4x8x&Highest resolution sprites to use: �Limit the maximum resolution to use for sprites. Limiting sprite resolution will avoid using high resolution graphics even when available. This can help keep the game appearance unified when using a mix of NewGRF files with and without high resolution graphics4x2x1xTown growth speed: Speed of town growthNoneSlowNormalFast	Very fast0Proportion of towns that will become cities: ^Amount of towns which will become a city, thus a town which starts out larger and grows faster1 in None!Initial city size multiplier: DAverage size of cities relative to normal towns at start of the game#Update distribution graph every ��Time between subsequent recalculations of the link graph. Each recalculation calculates the plans for one component of the graph. That means that a value X for this setting does not mean the whole graph will be updated every X seconds. Only some component will. The shorter you set it the more CPU time will be necessary to calculate it. The longer you set it the longer it will take until the cargo distribution starts on new routes0Take  for recalculation of distribution graph��Time taken for each recalculation of a link graph component. When a recalculation is started, a thread is spawned which is allowed to run for this number of seconds. The shorter you set this the more likely it is that the thread is not finished when it's supposed to. Then the game stops until it is ("lag"). The longer you set it the longer it takes for the distribution to be updated when routes change%Distribution mode for passengers: �"Symmetric" means that roughly the same number of passengers will go from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary numbers of passengers can go in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for passengersDistribution mode for mail: �"Symmetric" means that roughly the same amount of mail will be sent from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of mail can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for mail2Distribution mode for the ARMORED cargo class:  The ARMORED cargo class contains valuables in the temperate, diamonds in the subtropical, or gold in the subarctic climate. NewGRFs may change that. "Symmetric" means that roughly the same amount of that cargo will be sent from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of that cargo can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for that cargo. It is recommended to set this to asymmetric or manual when playing subarctic or subtropic, as banks only receive cargo in these climates. For temperate you can also choose symmetric as banks will send valuables back to the origin bank.Distribution mode for other cargo classes: �"Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of cargo can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for those cargosManual
Asymmetric	SymmetricDistribution accuracy: �The higher you set this the more CPU time the calculation of the link graph will take. If it takes too long you may notice lag. If you set it to a low value, however, the distribution will be inaccurate, and you may notice cargo not being sent to the places you expect it to go"Effect of distance on demands: �nIf you set this to a value higher than 0, the distance between the origin station A of some cargo and a possible destination B will have an effect on the amount of cargo sent from A to B. The further away B is from A the less cargo will be sent. The higher you set it, the less cargo will be sent to far away stations and the more cargo will be sent to near stations1Amount of returning cargo for symmetric mode: �Setting this to less than 100% makes the symmetric distribution behave more like the asymmetric one. Less cargo will be forcibly sent back if a certain amount is sent to a station. If you set it to 0% the symmetric distribution behaves just like the asymmetric one?Saturation of short paths before using high-capacity paths: ¿Frequently there are multiple paths between two given stations. Cargodist will saturate the shortest path first, then use the second shortest path until that is saturated and so on. Saturation is determined by an estimation of capacity and planned usage. Once it has saturated all paths, if there is still demand left, it will overload all paths, preferring the ones with high capacity. Most of the time the algorithm will not estimate the capacity accurately, though. This setting allows you to specify up to which percentage a shorter path must be saturated in the first pass before choosing the next longer one. Set it to less than 100% to avoid overcrowded stations in case of overestimated capacitySpeed units (land): Speed units (nautical): NWhenever a speed is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (mph)Metric (km/h)SI (m/s)Game units (tiles/day)Game units (tiles/sec)KnotsVehicle power units: XWhenever a vehicle's power is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (hp)Metric (hp)SI (kW)Weights units: OWhenever a weight is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (short t/ton)Metric (t/tonne)SI (kg)Volumes units: OWhenever a volume is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (gal)
Metric (l)SI (m³)Tractive effort units: wWhenever a tractive effort (also known as tractive force) is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (lbf)Metric (kgf)SI (kN)Heights units: OWhenever a height is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (ft)
